1
00:00:01,700 --> 00:00:03,800
You like living in New York, don't you?

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:05,100
Oh, I love it.

3
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:06,000
It's so convenient.

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:07,700
I can take the bus to work ...

5
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:08,600
or the subway ...

6
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:09,500
or a taxi.

7
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:11,900
And there's so much to do.

8
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:14,650
Lots of movie houses, and the theater.

9
00:00:14,650 --> 00:00:16,300
I know what you mean.

10
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:18,230
I'd like to live in the city,

11
00:00:18,230 --> 00:00:20,300
but living in New Jersey and the suburbs

12
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:21,600
is better for Michelle.

13
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:23,600
Trees, grass.

14
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:26,770
There's a lot of good things about suburban living.

15
00:00:26,770 --> 00:00:29,200
I grew up in Riverdale, remember?

16
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:30,300
So I know.

17
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:32,700
But, as a working woman,

18
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:34,500
I think New York has all the conveniences--

19
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:41,300
including the best tomatoes.

20
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:44,320
The truth is, I'd like to live in the city.

21
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:46,500
Michelle's the right age.

22
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:49,100
There are lots of things for her here.

23
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:50,100
You're right, Harry.

24
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:51,800
Today is the perfect example.

25
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:54,900
Michelle and her friends are at the aquarium in Brooklyn.

26
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:56,740
They come back here for lunch,

27
00:00:56,740 --> 00:00:59,400
then go uptown to the Museum of Natural History.

28
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:01,700
There's so much for young people to see and do.

29
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:03,200
It's just incredible!

30
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:03,800
Not just for young people.

31
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:05,000
What about me?

32
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:06,840
I've never been to the aquarium

33
00:01:06,840 --> 00:01:07,900
or the Museum of Natural History.

34
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:09,300
Have you?

35
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:09,700
Oh yes, Harry.

36
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:11,100
My mother and father

37
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:13,300
often took us somewhere in the city on the weekends.

38
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:14,600
Dad was a busy doctor,

39
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:17,600
but he usually managed to squeeze a Sunday in

40
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:18,800
with Richard, Robbie, and me.

41
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:22,400
I used to love to go to the Metropolitan Museum of Art.

42
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:23,000
I've been there several times.

43
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:25,500
Twice with Michelle.

44
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:28,500
You ought to think about spending more time with Michelle

45
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:30,500
at all the great places in the city.

46
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:33,200
Well, perhaps,

47
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:36,500
you'll help me select some of the great places.

48
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:41,800
And perhaps, you'll join us?

49
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:48,700
Perhaps I will.

50
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:50,000
Well, there we are.

51
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:51,700
All set for lunch, Harry.

52
00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:52,600
It looks inviting.

53
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:54,600
I wish Michelle and her friends would get here.

54
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:56,400
I'm starving, aren't you?

55
00:01:56,400 --> 00:01:59,100
I can't wait to take a bite of the pumpernickel.

56
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:01,750
It smells so delicious.

57
00:02:01,750 --> 00:02:06,500
Coming! Coming!

58
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:07,490
Who is it?

59
00:02:07,490 --> 00:02:09,500
Michelle.

60
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:10,500
It's us, Susan.

61
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:11,470
Come in, Michelle,

62
00:02:11,470 --> 00:02:12,690
and bring your friends along.

63
00:02:12,690 --> 00:02:14,300
We're on the fifth floor.

64
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:20,300
Oh, you've been here before.

65
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:22,700
Susan,

66
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:25,600
I really appreciate

67
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:27,000
your doing this for Michelle and her friends.

68
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:28,200
Oh, please, Harry.

69
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:30,100
It's nothing.

70
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:32,700
I'm not just doing it for Michelle.

71
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:36,100
I'm doing it for you.

72
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:39,000
Thank you.

73
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:44,600
I'm doing it for us, Harry.

74
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:45,100
Well,

75
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:48,100
it's ... it's important for Michelle to see us together more often.

76
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:50,200
That's true.

77
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:50,500
It's important

78
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:54,400
for Michelle and me to get to know each other better.

79
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:59,900
That's important for us.

80
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:02,400
That makes me feel good.

81
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:03,480
What, Harry?

82
00:03:03,480 --> 00:03:05,000
What makes you feel good?

83
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:06,600
Well, that you care about Michelle,

84
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:07,500
that you care about me, and

85
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:12,600
that you care about us.

86
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:16,200
Well, Harry, that's because I do.

87
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:21,200
I do care.

88
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:22,800
They'll be here any minute.

89
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:29,700
Susan, I'd like to continue this conversation later.

90
00:03:29,700 --> 00:03:31,200
Of course, Harry.

91
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:35,400
We'll finish the conversation when they go to the museum.

92
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:37,600
I'd like that.

93
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:40,600
So would I.
